AU  - Gregoriou, A
TI  - Can corruption favour growth via the composition of government spending?
T2  - ECONOMICS BULLETIN
AB  - In an endogenous growth model with two public goods, we analytically derive the optimal composition of government spending in the presence of corruption. Although corruption results in a loss of productivity per se, an increase in corruption in the category of public spending that is harmed relatively more by corruption could have a favourable effect on growth, as it would encourage a benevolent government to divert spending towards the public good that is more productive, net of corruption.
