TI  - Benefit sharing: An incentive mechanism for social control of government expenditure
T2  - QUARTERLY REVIEW OF ECONOMICS AND FINANCE
AB  - The present paper analyzes the incentives individual members of society face to contribute to a nation's efforts in controlling corruption. A Principal-Agent model is constructed, leading to the following results. First, although individual agents do have an interest in devoting a portion of their resources to the nation's control effort, the opportunity cost of the effort and a free rider problem blocks the spontaneous provision of individual support to corruption control. Second, to cope with those incentives, a new welfare improving mechanism is proposed, which aligns individual incentives with those of society at no extra cost to the government. (C) 2007 Board of Trustees of the University of Illinois. Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.
