TY  - JOUR
AU  - Ansolabehere, S
TI  - The Scope of Corruption: Lessons from Comparative Campaign Finance Disclosure
T2  - ELECTION LAW JOURNAL
AB  - This article describes two systems within which campaign finance laws seek to limit corruption. One consists of the legal and ethical rules governing relations between individual politicians and donors. The other is the macro-level regulation of total campaign spending and the acceptable level of private influence over the public sector. Disclosure is integral to both. Looking at four distinct systems, the U. S., the U. K., Germany, and California, I assess the scope of corruption at the micro (legal) and macro (regulatory) levels. To assess micro level evidence of corruption, I focus on the U.S. and California. These two systems have long experience with independent enforcement agencies. Both show evidence of effective and active enforcement of the laws, with violations of campaign finance laws on a very small scale. To assess the macro scale of corruption, I compare the growth of spending in all four systems. All four show an important regularity. Campaign spending is a very small fraction of national income (and of government spending) and it grows at the same rate of income. These facts are consistent with the view that corruption through campaign spending is a small societal problem. I conclude with a comment on why we should have disclosure, even though corruption in this system appears to be on a very small scale.
