TY  - JOUR
AU  - Garmaise, MJ
AU  - Natividad, G
TI  - Fiscal windfalls and entrepreneurship: fostering entry or promoting incumbents?
T2  - SMALL BUSINESS ECONOMICS
AB  - We study the impact of government fiscal windfalls on entrepreneurship through an analysis of shocks to transfer payments in Peru. These pure transfers generate higher government spending, and we contrast the impacts on incumbent firms and entrants. Incumbent exits from the financial system are reduced, but previously troubled firms experience deteriorating performance. Increased transfers result in higher rates of firm entry into formal borrowing and superior entrant outcomes. Overall, startups outperform non-startups after heightened transfers.
   Plain English SummaryWhen local governments receive larger transfers from a national government, young firms benefit more than established incumbents. We show that local government fiscal windfalls in Peru lead to higher local government spending. Higher transfers reduce the likelihood that existing firms will stop borrowing from lenders. Higher transfers also spur greater entry into credit markets for new firms. Overall, startups outperform non-startups when transfer payments are high. Transfer payments therefore offer a powerful mechanism for governments to encourage local entrepreneurship.
