AB  - This article examines economic development from 1996 to 2015 for 192 countries and specifically Latin America. Evidence shows that each 0.1-point increase in institutions impacts a 3.9% improvement in Latin American per capita output versus a 2.6% effect on world development. This new evidence from Latin America shows a missing opportunity to develop at higher annual pace than the 2.14% average, mainly due to the deterioration in rule of law. We conjecture the efficiency of monetary/fiscal policies will improve if policy makers emphasize projects that foster improvements to institutional quality, such as transparency, public spending quality and fiscal responsibility. (C) 2018 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
