TY  - JOUR
AU  - Corrêa, IM
AU  - Spinelli, MVC
TI  - Transparency Policies in Brazilian Public Administration
T2  - REVISTA DEL CLAD REFORMA Y DEMOCRACIA
AB  - The quest for public transparency has been one of the leading topics of discussion in contemporary democracies. Transparently opening to the whole population how public money has been used, not only properly managing it, has become, besides a challenge, a key action to be adopted by those heads of government called "modern" and "democratic".
   In Brazil, especially over the last decade, many initiatives were taken in pursuit of enhanced transparency. In this study, it is attempted to discuss the evolution of these initiatives through the enactment of laws that forced the publication of technical reports containing either financial or budgetary data, the adoption of electronic public procurement procedures, and the use of Internet portals with non-restricted access to citizens, where any public spending can be easily queried.
   In addition, it is the intention to demonstrate that the Fiscal Responsibility Act, the electronic bidding process, the portals and pages of transparency of the Executive branch, the Federal Government's management system on voluntary transfers of resources, the use of corporate cards by public officials for small payments, the divulgence of a list of companies barred from contracting by the public sector and the referral to Congress of a bill concerning issues related to access to information, all together were initiatives implemented in an environment characterized by the quest for enhanced transparency, which, over the last decade, has been growing in Brazil.
   The adoption of such initiatives allowed the authors to conclude that the path towards public transparency is not simple because it involves a number of factors, ranging from the integration of complex systems and the provision of public access to official documents to the translation of technical language into a friendly language, as well as the need to foster the awareness and learning of citizens in relation to this subject.
   Despite these difficulties, the immeasurable benefits arising from the adoption of mechanisms for public transparency have become evident for both government and society. Disclosing in details how public money is being applied and either enabling or encouraging the society's control over the acts of government is the clearest demonstration of confidence in what is being done. Far from being a guarantee of the extermination of harmful practices to the Treasury, it is a basic condition for the exercise of full citizenship for fighting corruption, improving public management and, ultimately, promoting inclusion and social justice. It is therefore much more than a wish of a specific head of government or a practice that should not be continued due to ideological or partisan differences. It is a challenge and a duty which applies to all those governments that claim to be democratic.
